        ],
        'content' => '<p class="lead">The <strong>car loan interest deduction</strong> is a new tax break created by the One Big Beautiful Bill (OBBBA) that lets qualifying taxpayers deduct interest paid on a loan for a new vehicle, up to $10,000 a year. It runs for tax years 2025 through 2028 and is claimed on the new Schedule 1-A attached to Form 1040. This guide walks through who qualifies, the income phase-out, the US-assembly requirement, and a worked example. For related financing questions, see our guide on <a href="/should-i-refinance-my-car/">whether to refinance your car loan</a>.</p>'
            . '<p><em>The Treasury Department and IRS issued proposed regulations on this deduction on December 31, 2025 (IR-2025-129), with a public comment period that ran through February 2, 2026. The income phase-out thresholds below are confirmed in IRS Publication 6126. A handful of narrower procedural details are still being finalized; check the final Schedule 1-A instructions on IRS.gov before you file, and this is not personal tax advice.</em></p>'
            . '<h2>What is the car loan interest deduction?</h2><p>According to the IRS, the deduction applies to interest on vehicle loans incurred after December 31, 2024, to purchase new made-in-America vehicles for personal use. Two points stand out from the IRS\'s own description. First, it covers only <strong>new</strong> vehicles, not used cars. Second, the vehicle must have its final assembly in the United States, and the proposed regulations lay out how that is determined. The IRS also confirms the deduction "applies to both taxpayers who take the standard deduction and those who itemize deductions," but it does not reduce your adjusted gross income (AGI); it is reported separately on Schedule 1-A and reduces your taxable income whether or not you itemize.</p>'
            . '<h2>Who qualifies: eligibility checklist</h2><ul><li><strong>The loan must be for a new vehicle.</strong> Used vehicles and most leases are generally described as not qualifying in IRS and secondary guidance.</li><li><strong>The loan must have been taken out after December 31, 2024.</strong> Interest on older loans is not covered.</li><li><strong>The vehicle\'s final assembly must be in the United States.</strong> You can verify this yourself using the VIN or window sticker, as described below.</li><li><strong>The vehicle must be for personal use, not business use.</strong> The proposed regulations define how personal use is determined.</li><li><strong>Your income must be below, or only modestly above, the phase-out threshold.</strong> IRS Publication 6126 confirms the phase-out starts at $100,000 of modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) for single filers and $200,000 for joint filers, reduced by $200 for every $1,000 (or part of $1,000) of MAGI above the threshold.</li><li><strong>The deduction applies only for tax years 2025 through 2028,</strong> per the structure described in IRS and secondary guidance on the One Big Beautiful Bill.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Phase-out: how the income limit works</h2><p>Multiple tax-preparation sources describe the same phase-out mechanism: the deduction is reduced by $200 for every $1,000 (or portion of $1,000) that your MAGI exceeds the threshold. The table below uses the commonly reported thresholds. Check the final Schedule 1-A instructions for any last-minute change before you file.</p>'
