            . '<tr><td>Married filing jointly</td><td>$200,000</td><td>$200 reduction per $1,000 of MAGI over $200,000</td><td>Around $250,000 (sources vary between about $249,000 and $250,000)</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table>'
            . '<p>Worked phase-out example: a single filer with $10,000 of qualifying interest and MAGI of $115,000 is $15,000 over the $100,000 threshold. At $200 per $1,000 over, that is 15 x $200 = $3,000 of reduction, leaving a deduction of about $7,000 instead of the full $10,000. A joint filer with the same $10,000 of interest and MAGI of $210,000 is $10,000 over the $200,000 threshold, a reduction of 10 x $200 = $2,000, leaving about $8,000. These are illustrations based on the confirmed IRS thresholds; your actual deduction may differ slightly once the final Schedule 1-A instructions are published.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: how much could you actually save?</h2><p>Assume a taxpayer finances a new, US-assembled vehicle with a loan that generates $9,500 of interest in the first full year, below the $10,000 cap. If this taxpayer is single with MAGI of $90,000, under the threshold, the full $9,500 could be deductible (subject to final rules). In the 22% federal marginal bracket, that is roughly $2,090 of tax savings for the year. If the same taxpayer\'s MAGI were $130,000 instead, the reported phase-out would cut the deduction by 30 x $200 = $6,000, leaving about $3,500 deductible, worth roughly $770 at a 22% marginal rate. As loan balances amortize, the interest portion of each payment shrinks every year, so the dollar value of the deduction is largest in the loan\'s early years. Estimate your own numbers with our <a href="{tool:auto-loan-calculator}">auto loan calculator</a> and <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Does the loan have to be secured by the vehicle, or can it be a refinance?</h2><p>The proposed regulations describe a requirement that the loan be a first lien on the vehicle, meaning the vehicle itself secures the loan. Two points are still being finalized: whether refinancing an existing qualifying loan preserves eligibility, and whether a loan not secured by the vehicle (such as some personal loans used to buy a car) can qualify. Check the final Schedule 1-A instructions or a tax professional on these two points before filing.</p>'
            . '<h2>Which vehicles qualify, and how do you check final assembly?</h2><p>The deduction covers cars, SUVs, vans, pickup trucks and motorcycles under 14,000 pounds gross vehicle weight rating, as long as the vehicle is new and your personal-use loan was taken out after December 31, 2024. The brand does not decide eligibility; some foreign-brand vehicles assembled in US plants can qualify, while some vehicles from US-headquartered automakers assembled abroad cannot. To check a specific vehicle, look at the window sticker or the manufacturer\'s label (often inside the driver-side door), which lists the final assembly location, or decode the VIN using NHTSA\'s free VIN decoder at vpic.nhtsa.dot.gov. Keep a record of the VIN, since it generally needs to be reported on Schedule 1-A each year you claim the deduction.</p>'
            . '<h2>How lenders report car loan interest to the IRS</h2><p>IRS Publication 6127 describes new reporting rules for lenders. Beginning with the 2026 calendar year, a business that receives $600 or more in interest on a qualified passenger vehicle loan from an individual in a calendar year generally must file an information return. This is commonly referred to in secondary coverage as Form 1098 vehicle loan interest (VLI) reporting. In practice, this means many borrowers should receive a statement from their lender listing the qualifying interest paid during the year, which should simplify filling out Schedule 1-A.</p>'
            . '<h2>How to claim the car loan interest deduction</h2><ol><li><strong>Confirm the vehicle and loan qualify:</strong> new vehicle, loan dated after December 31, 2024, final assembly in the United States, and personal use.</li><li><strong>Gather your interest paid for the year,</strong> ideally from a lender statement (Form 1098 VLI reporting begins for the 2026 calendar year; for the 2025 tax year you may need to request the figure directly from your lender).</li><li><strong>Calculate your MAGI</strong> to see whether the phase-out reduces your deduction.</li><li><strong>Complete Schedule 1-A (Form 1040)</strong> and carry the result to your Form 1040 as instructed.</li><li><strong>Keep records</strong> of the loan agreement, vehicle purchase documents, and interest statements in case the IRS asks for substantiation.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2><ul><li><strong>Assuming a used car qualifies.</strong> Guidance consistently describes the deduction as limited to new vehicles.</li><li><strong>Assuming any American brand qualifies.</strong> The requirement is about where final assembly happened, not the brand name, so even some vehicles from US-headquartered automakers may not qualify if assembled abroad, and some foreign-brand vehicles assembled in the US may qualify.</li><li><strong>Forgetting the income phase-out.</strong> Higher earners may see a reduced deduction or none at all once reported thresholds are confirmed.</li><li><strong>Double-counting with other benefits.</strong> This deduction is about loan interest, not the vehicle\'s purchase price, sales tax, or any separate vehicle-related credit.</li><li><strong>Not checking final guidance before filing.</strong> The core phase-out thresholds are IRS-confirmed, but a few procedural details are still being finalized, so check the Schedule 1-A instructions before filing.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/should-i-refinance-my-car/">Should I refinance my car loan?</a></li><li><a href="/cash-back-or-low-apr-car-deal/">Cash back or low APR: which car deal saves more?</a></li><li><a href="/negative-equity-car-loan-how-to-get-out/">Negative equity on a car loan: how to get out</a></li><li><a href="/year-end-tax-planning-checklist/">Year-end tax planning checklist</a></li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/node/153796" rel="noopener" target="_blank">news release IR-2025-129, Treasury and IRS guidance on the car loan interest deduction</a> (Dec. 31, 2025); IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/p6126.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Publication 6126</a> (car loan interest deduction, including the MAGI phase-out thresholds); IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-pdf/p6127.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Publication 6127</a> (lender reporting of vehicle loan interest); IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/prior-year-forms-and-instructions" rel="noopener" target="_blank">prior-year forms and instructions</a> (Schedule 1-A listing); IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/draft-tax-forms" rel="noopener" target="_blank">draft tax forms</a> page; NHTSA <a href="https://vpic.nhtsa.dot.gov/decoder/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">VIN decoder</a>. A few finer eligibility details (the lien/refinance treatment) are still pending final regulations; verify them against the final Schedule 1-A instructions before filing.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax or financial advice.</p>'
